                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">6,500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120/240V 30A twist-lock</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Inverter</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Read the 240 Volt Amperage</h3>

          <p>
            For home backup, the 240 volt amperage is the most honest number on the spec sheet. It tells you how much power can travel through the twist-lock, the cord and the inlet into your panel. Multiply it by 240 and you get the running watts the house will actually see: about 6,500 for the Generac, 5,500 for the Honda and 5,100 for the WEN. That number decides which of the plans later on this page you can run.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Which Meaning This Page Covers</h3>

          <p>
            This page covers the whole 6,500 watt label, both meanings. If you already know you only need about 5,000 running watts for a short list of essentials, our guide to the <a href="/best-5000-watt-generator/">best 5000 watt generators</a> has lighter, cheaper picks. If you want a well pump and a window air conditioner running at the same time, the <a href="/best-8000-watt-generator/">8000 watt class</a> is the better fit.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="house-plan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Can a 6500 Watt Generator Run a House? Three Load Plans
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 6,500 watt generator runs the essentials of most houses through a transfer switch or interlock, but not the whole house at once, and not central air or an electric range. The useful question is which essentials, and how many motors can start at the same time. The plans below use typical running and starting figures from our <a href="/generator-wattage-chart/">generator wattage chart</a>; your appliances may differ, and their labels or a clamp meter are the only way to know.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan 1: Fridge, freezer, furnace and lights</h3>

          <p>
            A refrigerator runs around 700 watts, a chest freezer around 500, a gas furnace blower around 800, and lights, a router, a TV and phone chargers maybe 500. That is about 2,500 running watts. The largest motor start is the furnace blower or the fridge compressor, around 2,000 to 2,400 watts for a second. Either kind of 6,500 watt generator runs this plan with room to spare, and so does a 5,000 watt generator. If this is your whole list, you do not need the Generac.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan 2: Add a sump pump and a microwave</h3>

          <p>
            A half-horsepower sump pump adds roughly 1,000 running watts and a start near 2,000; a microwave adds about 1,000 to 1,200 while it runs. The running total climbs to around 4,500 to 5,000 watts with everything on. A 5,000 running-watt generator like the Briggs Q6500 or WEN DF680iX is now at its limit, and you will be turning things off to use the microwave. The Generac GP6500 and Champion 201169 still have about 1,500 watts of headroom.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plan 3: Add a 240 volt well pump</h3>

          <p>
            A well pump is the load that separates the two kinds of 6,500 watt generator. A typical one-horsepower submersible pump runs around 1,500 to 2,000 watts at 240 volts, but its starting draw can be two to three times that for a moment, often 4,000 watts or more. Add that start on top of the 2,500 watts of Plan 1 and the generator has to supply roughly 6,500 to 7,000 watts for a second or two. A generator with 8,125 starting watts does that comfortably. One with 6,500 starting watts may trip its breaker or stall the pump, especially an inverter, whose electronics limit surge more strictly than an open-frame alternator. If you have a well, buy a generator with 6,500 running watts, or stage the start by turning the fridge and freezer off for the moment the pump kicks on.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Size by running watts for the list, and by starting watts for the biggest motor.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Add up running watts for everything that will be on at once, then add the starting surge of the single largest motor. That total must fit under the generator's starting rating, and the running total must fit under its running rating.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Our companion guide to <a href="/what-will-a-5000-watt-generator-run/">what a 5000 watt generator will run</a> works through load lists for the 5,000 watt class in more detail, including how to stage motor starts. The short version for this size: a 5,000 running-watt machine runs Plans 1 and 2 with care, and a 6,500 running-watt machine runs all three.
          </p>
        </div>

