        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Placement, Weight and Starting
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Weight and Moving It</h3>

          <p>
            A 6,500 watt generator is a heavy machine, and how you move and start it matters more in an outage than in a showroom. The open frames weigh 172 to 206 pounds and the Honda around 260. All of them have wheels and a handle, but you will be pushing them across a lawn or down a driveway, so plan a storage spot close to where they will run, and a level pad for them to sit on.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Placement</h3>

          <p>
            Place it at least 20 feet from the house with the exhaust pointed away, which is the distance safety agencies commonly cite, and never in a garage, even with the door open. Every generator here has carbon monoxide shutdown, but that protects the person standing beside the generator, not the people inside the house where exhaust can drift through a window. Our guide on <a href="/where-to-place-a-generator-outside/">where to place a generator outside</a> covers spacing, covers and rain.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Electric Start</h3>

          <p>
            Electric start is worth more than it looks. The Champion, WEN and Honda start with a button, and pulling a recoil cord on a 389cc engine on a cold morning is hard work for many people. If you choose the Generac or Briggs, both recoil start, practice before the outage and keep the engine maintained so it starts on the first few pulls. For the electric-start models, keep the battery on a maintainer between uses; a dead starter battery is a common reason generators fail on the first day of a storm.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Exercise and Storage</h3>

          <p>
            Run the generator under load for 20 to 30 minutes every few months, and treat stored gasoline with stabilizer or run the carburetor dry before storage. The Honda's fuel injection makes it the most forgiving here, but none of them are maintenance-free.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Who Should Buy a Different Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            A 6,500 watt generator is the right size for most homes that want the essentials through a transfer switch, but not for everyone. If your list is just a refrigerator, a freezer, the furnace blower and lights, with no well and no sump pump, a 5,000 watt generator is lighter and cheaper. If you need a well pump, a window air conditioner and the essentials all running together, the 8,000 watt class has the headroom.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have a 50-amp transfer switch or want to run central air, a 6,500 watt generator is too small. Central air conditioners need 3,000 to 5,000 running watts and a large start, which means 10,000 running watts or more and a 50-amp outlet. If you are not sure where your house falls, start with our <a href="/best-portable-generator/">best portable generators</a> guide, which covers every size. If you mostly need quiet power for a camper or a few devices, a small inverter is a better buy than anything on this page.
          </p>
        </div>

