            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a 6500 watt generator run on a tank of gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generac rates the GP6500's 7.9-gallon tank at 11.5 hours at half load and 16.5 hours at quarter load. Champion rates the 201169 at about 12 hours at half load. Inverters run longer at light loads; Honda rates the EU7000iS at about 18 hours at quarter load on 5.1 gallons. Heavier loads shorten every figure.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a 6500 watt generator need a 30 or 50 amp transfer switch?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 30-amp switch. Every generator on this list has a 30-amp, 120/240 volt twist-lock as its largest outlet, and none has a 50-amp outlet. A 30-amp circuit at 240 volts carries up to 7,200 watts, which covers even a 6,500 running-watt generator. A 50-amp switch costs more and would never be fully used.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much power do you lose running a 6500 watt generator on propane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically 8% to 12%. The Champion 201169 drops from 6,500 to 6,000 running watts on propane and to 5,500 on natural gas. The WEN DF680iX drops from 5,100 to 4,500 running watts. If you plan to run on propane most of the time, compare generators by their propane ratings rather than their gasoline numbers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a 6500 watt generator enough for central air?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally no. A central air conditioner typically needs 3,000 to 5,000 running watts and a large starting surge, and it is usually wired on a 240 volt circuit that a 30-amp switch is not sized for alongside the essentials. Running central air on a portable generally means a 10,000 running-watt-plus generator, a 50-amp outlet and often a soft starter on the compressor.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing peak watts instead of running watts</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The 6,500 on the box is often a starting figure. A generator rated 6,500 peak and 5,000 running is a 5,000 watt generator for everything except a one-second motor start. Compare running watts on the fuel you will use, and the 240 volt amperage, before comparing prices.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a 5,000 running-watt model for a well pump</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A one-horsepower well pump can need 4,000 watts or more for a moment as it starts. Added to a running fridge, freezer and furnace, that can exceed a 6,500 watt surge rating, especially on an inverter. If you have a well, buy a generator with around 8,000 starting watts or plan to stage the pump start.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the propane derate</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every dual and tri fuel generator makes less power on propane. On the WEN DF680iX it is 600 running watts, which turns a 5,100 watt generator into a 4,500 watt one. If you plan to run mainly on propane, size the generator by its propane rating, not the gasoline number on the box.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Overloading one leg of the panel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 120/240 volt generator splits its power across two legs. Put all your 120 volt circuits on one leg and that leg trips at half the generator's rating. Ask the electrician to balance circuits across both legs, or use a transfer switch with watt meters so you can see the load on each side.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Underestimating fuel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An open-frame 6,500 watt generator at half load burns about 0.7 gallons an hour, more than 16 gallons a day if it runs around the clock. Plan fuel storage for the outage you expect, run in cycles to stretch it, and consider natural gas or propane for long outages.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

